L32: ## 【15†noun】\nL33: \nL34: ar·​mi·​stice 【16†ˈär-mə-stəs 】\nL35: \nL36: 【17†Synonyms of armistice】\nL37: \nL38: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL39: \nL40: : temporary stopping of open acts of warfare by agreement between the opponents : 【18†truce】\nL41: \nL42: 【19†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL43: ## Did you know?\nL44: Armistice comes from the New Latin word armistitium, which in turn combines a stem of the Latin verb sistere, meaning \"to make stand, halt, bring to a standstill,\" with arma, meaning \"implements of war, weapons.\" An armistice, therefore, is literally a cessation of arms. 【20†Armistice Day†www.britannica.com】 is the name that was given to the holiday celebrated in the United States on November 11 before it was renamed 【21†Veterans Day†www.britannica.com】 by Congress in 1954.\nL45: The original name refers to the agreement between the 【22†Allied】 Powers and Germany to end the hostilities that constituted the First World War—an agreement designated to take effect on the eleventh hour of the eleventh day of the eleventh month.\nL46: ## Synonyms of armistice\nL47: \nL48: Relevance\nL49: \nL50:   * 【18†truce】\nL51:   * 【23†ceasefire】\nL52: \nL53: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL54: ## Examples of armistice in a Sentence\nL55: \nL56: both sides in the conflict agreed to an armistice during the solemn holy days\nL57: \nL58: Recent Examples on the Web\nL59: \nL60: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【24†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL61: Hays was discharged soon after the armistice that ended World War I. — Arkansas Online, 31 Oct. 2026 The two countries are separated by the Demilitarized Zone that was established when the Korean War ended in an armistice in 1953. — Reuters, NBC news, 21 Oct. 2026 The zone is a legacy of the 1950-53 Korean War, which ended with an armistice rather than a peace treaty. — ABC News, 28 Sep.\nL62: 2026 North and South Korea are still formally at war, as the Korean War stopped in 1953 with a armistice and not a peace treaty. — Lim Hui Jie, CNBC, 23 Sep. 2026 【25†See All Example Sentences for armistice】\nL63: ## Word History\nL64: \nL65: Etymology\nL66: \nL67: borrowed from New Latin armistitium, from Latin arma \"implements of war, weapons\" + -stit-, -stes (going back to *-sta-t-s, root noun derivative from Indo-European *steh_{2}- the base of Latin sistere \"to make stand, halt, bring to a standstill,\" stāre \"to stand\") + -ium, suffix of compounded nouns — more at 【26†arm entry 3】, 【27†stand entry 1】\nL68: Note: The Dictionary of Medieval Latin from British Sources records armistitium from medieval Scottish documents preserved in England (Rotuli Scotiae in Turri Londensi et in Domo Capitulari Westmonasteriensi asservati, vol. 1, London, 1814, p. 335). However, the word occurs only in the text of a heading summarizing the contents of a letter written in April, 1335. These headings were presumably composed when the documents were collected for publication and do not reflect medieval usage of armistitium.\nL69: Printed records of the word are in abundance only after 1610, when it appears in the dedicatory preface to Biblical commentaries by the French Jesuit Nicolaus Serarius (In sacros divinorum bibliorum libros, Tobiam, Iudith, Esther et Machabaeos commentarius, Mainz, 1610), though there is no reason to believe Serarius coined it. The model for the coinage may have been Latin solstitium 【28†solstice】.\nL70: First Known Use\nL71: \nL72: 1677, in the meaning defined 【29†above】\nL73: \nL74: Time Traveler\nL75: \nL76: The first known use of armistice was in 1677\nL77: \nL78: 【30†See more words from the same year】\nL79: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL80: \nL81: 【31†armipotent】\nL82: \nL83: armistice\nL84: \nL85: 【32†Armistice Day】\n